WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:36.460
‘The art of war is of vital importance to
the State.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:41.250
It is a matter of life and death, a road either
to safety or to ruin.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:45.890
Hence, it is a subject of inquiry which can
on no account be neglected’

00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:50.680
These are the first sentences of the Sunzi
Bingfa, known in the West as The Art of War,

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:55.570
one of the oldest treatises on strategy and
warfare and for sure, the most quoted across

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:56.570
the Globe.

00:00:56.570 --> 00:01:00.850
Its 13 chapters offer high level strategic
advice to rulers and generals, focusing on

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:06.619
a series of key principles which should always
be kept in mind, lest one takes the road to

00:01:06.619 --> 00:01:07.619
ruin.

00:01:07.619 --> 00:01:10.660
Some of its commandments are very specific
to warfare in the ancient world, for example

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:14.530
chapter 12 is entirely dedicated to Attack
by Fire.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:19.520
But most of its teachings can be extrapolated
to a variety of situations outside of the

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:23.610
military environment, including politics,
finance, marketing or even sports.

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:30.790
But what do we know about its author, the
legendary Sun Tzu, also known as Sunzi?

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:33.840
Surprisingly little, for a man whose work
has had such an enduring influence on the

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:37.520
politics and History of China at first and
then the whole World.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:42.250
But in today’s Biographics we will attempt
to clear at least some of the mist shrouding

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:46.000
the life of Master Sun.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:51.159
The Seven Kingdoms

00:01:51.159 --> 00:01:55.630
To fully understand the man Sun and the profound
legacy of his work, we first need to look

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:58.090
at the historical context in which they appeared.

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:05.570
The years between 770BC and 476BC are known
as “The Spring and Autumn Period of China”.

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:09.160
Over these three centuries, the land was ruled
by the Zhou dinasty.

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:14.290
The Zhous applied a feudal-like system, with
large swathes of land administered by local

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:15.290
lords.

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:19.860
If a disagreement broke out, the lords would
settle them by means of small scale military

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:25.230
engagements, waged using bronze weapons, riding
on top of richly decorated chariots.

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:29.610
These battles, which actually were more like
large duels, were regulated by a code of honour

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:34.890
and valour, based on the philosophy of Confucianism,
which values strict adherence to a system

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:37.090
of traditions and social rules.

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:40.670
Here is an example, narrated by historian
Robert Griffiths:

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:43.830
“In ancient China war had been regarded
as a knightly contest.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:49.310
As such, it had been governed by a code to
which both sides generally adhered.

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:54.171
Many illustrations of this are found…For
example, in 632 BC the Chin commander, after

00:02:54.171 --> 00:02:59.110
defeating Ch’u at Ch’eng P’u, gave the
vanquished enemy three days’ supply of food.

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:03.670
This courtesy was later reciprocated by a
Ch’u army victorious at Pi”

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:08.860
This chivalrous approach to war was made possible
by the small size of the armies and the low

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:09.860
stakes at play.

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:15.060
But the Zhou dynasty was on the decline until
they were removed from power and things changed

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:16.060
for the worse.

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:21.830
A vacuum at the top normally causes the emergence
of regional powers, all vying for supremacy.

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:26.900
And this is what happened in China during
the so-called Period of the Warring States.

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:33.270
Seven main Kingdoms, Jin, Chu, Qin, Qi, Wei,
Yue and Wu, alongside their vassal states,

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:36.970
were at war for much of the VIth and Vth century
BC.

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:39.230
Think Westeros after King Robert’s death.

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:41.240
Or Westeros, after King Joffrey’s death.

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:42.640
Or Westeros, after King Tonmen’s – you
got the idea.

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:45.800
Obviously this constant state of conflict
had a massive impact on society.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:49.830
The rulers of the Seven Kingdoms could not
afford to go to war on fancy bronze chariots,

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:52.970
but most of all they could not afford to lose.

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:57.930
And so bronze weapons were replaced by iron,
cheaper but deadlier.

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:01.700
Chariots were discarded, in favour of cavalry
and infantry units.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:05.630
The warriors, aristocrats and heroes who rode
into battle during the Spring and Autumn,

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:11.030
gave way to large armies of conscripts numbering
in the tens, even hundreds of thousands.

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:14.690
And large numbers of well-equipped, well-fed
men require tons of money.

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:16.830
Who’s going to pay?

00:04:16.830 --> 00:04:22.950
As usual, the peasants, the merchants, ordinary
folk, hard hit by taxation that depletes the

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:24.680
land and sows dissent.

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:28.680
The Kings of the Warring States need some
expert advice: not only on how to overcome

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:31.610
their foes, but how to do it efficiently and
quickly.

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:35.170
But who can provide it?

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:43.350
Enter the Master
Sun Wu, later known as Sun Tzu, or ‘Master

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:49.730
Sun’ was born in 544BC in the northern state
of Ch’i, modern day province of Shandong.

00:04:49.730 --> 00:04:54.000
But please don’t take this date too seriously:
the exact Century in which the Master live

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:57.220
is disputed, let alone the year.

00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:01.280
But let’s settle for 544.

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:05.910
He was obviously literate, he knew one or
two things about war and leadership and he

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:10.080
travelled across the Warring States, which
leads us to infer that he was a shih, or a

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:12.260
member of the landless aristocracy.

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:16.910
The shih’s were traditionally travelling
academics, but Sun Wu was more likely a travelling

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:17.910
mercenary.

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:21.581
It was this particular trade that took him
to travel from his birth state of Ch’i to

00:05:21.581 --> 00:05:25.800
the southern kingdom of Wu, sometime around
510BC.

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:31.120
The state of Wu was at that time a sort of
‘underdog’ in the Chinese Game of Thrones.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:35.020
This kingdom is located in what is now the
Shanghai province and back at that time it

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:39.700
was smaller and less populated then its rivals,
especially the large, neighbouring state of

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:40.840
Chu.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:45.730
That is why, the ruler King He-Lu, was looking
for a top-notch military advisor to organise

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:48.200
and lead his army in war.

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:51.480
According to some accounts, at this stage
Master Sun had already written the Art of

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:54.250
War, leading to He-Lu summoning him to court.

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:57.460
According to others, Sun Wu was only a low
ranking officer in He-Lu’s army at this

00:05:57.460 --> 00:06:03.020
point, but his skills in unconventional tactics
such as ambushes, espionage and counter-espionage,

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:06.370
surprise attacks on enemy camps, had raised
the attention of the King.

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:09.270
But what everybody agrees on is how he got
the General job.

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:14.470
King He-Lu challenged Sun Wu to demonstrate
his military skills by organising some war

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:18.630
manoeuvres, not with soldiers, but with the
court concubines.

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:23.490
Sun Wu accepted the challenge, on two conditions:
no interference from the King and absolute

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:25.780
obedience from the concubines.

00:06:25.780 --> 00:06:30.401
Sun then proceeded to created two platoons
of 70 or 80 concubines each, appointing as

00:06:30.401 --> 00:06:33.051
platoon leaders the two favourites of the
King.

00:06:33.051 --> 00:06:37.700
He then instructed the concubines on how to
perform a series of marching orders in coordination

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:38.700
with a drum beat.

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:42.790
But when the drums sounded, the two lead concubines
burst out laughing and the two platoons did

00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:44.290
not obey the orders.

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:46.830
Sun Wu was like ‘OK, that’s cool, it can
happen.

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:51.380
If at first the officers and the soldiers
do not perform correctly, it is the General’s

00:06:51.380 --> 00:06:52.540
fault, my fault, for not being clear enough’.

00:06:52.540 --> 00:06:56.550
And again Sun gave the marching orders to
the concubines, making it very simple and

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:57.950
clear what is it they had to do.

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:02.120
And again, the drums went off … and again
the two favourites just couldn’t stop giggling.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:03.120
How fun, right?

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:04.120
Not exactly.

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:07.120
At this point Sun Wu commented that he, the
General, had done his duty, it was now the

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:10.290
fault of the officers if they were not executing
the orders.

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:14.169
To show that he meant business he ordered
two guardsmen armed with axes to apprehend

00:07:14.169 --> 00:07:15.480
the two concubines.

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:19.590
He-Lu, watching at a distance with his court,
dispatched a message asking him to stop.

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:21.790
“Please Sun Wu, do not chop these ladies’
heads off.

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:28.630
My life would be so not fun without them”
To which Master Sun replied that when a General

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:31.130
is in charge his orders must be carried out.

00:07:31.130 --> 00:07:33.980
The axemen decapitated the two squad leaders.

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:38.380
The deputy-lead concubines were put in charge
of the platoons and the manoeuvres were carried

00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:39.380
out perfectly.

00:07:39.380 --> 00:07:43.150
I guess threat of decapitation is a powerful
incentive.

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:48.350
King He-Lu in the end was kind of cool with
all that … so much so that he appointed

00:07:48.350 --> 00:07:52.080
Master Sun as lead General of his army.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:54.510
Now a couple of points if I may.

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:58.180
Point one: we all have a lot to learn from
Master Sun and his book.

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:00.800
But I would not take his advice when it comes
to job interviews.

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:05.610
Chopping the head off the CEO’s life partners
will not get you that dream job.

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:10.680
Point two: I personally find it difficult
to believe this to be a true story, and XIth

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:12.550
century scholar Yeh Cheng-Tse agrees with
me.

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:16.790
This sounds more like a parable to illustrate
some key lessons which appear again and again

00:08:16.790 --> 00:08:17.990
in the Art of War.

00:08:17.990 --> 00:08:19.710
What are the lessons?

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:22.540
Absolute obedience to the officers and the
Generals.

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:23.540
Discipline.

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:24.540
Coordination among ranks.

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:27.080
Independence of the Military from political
power in war time.

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:34.000
These are the values that make an army of
men into an army of heroes.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:09:47.240
One Sun, two Suns, three Suns … ?
But what exactly was so revolutionary about

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:49.100
Sun Tzu’s approach to warfare?

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:54.279
His main contribution was to apply Taoist
principles to the conduct of military campaigns.

00:09:54.279 --> 00:09:59.690
Unlike the adherence to tradition supported
by Confucianism, Taoism emphasizes adaptability

00:09:59.690 --> 00:10:01.580
to the natural flow of things.

00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:06.910
Moreover, the Tao-Te-Ching reflects a horror
of war and yearning for peace.

00:10:06.910 --> 00:10:12.290
By combining these two principles, you obtain
the foundations of Sun Tzu’s military doctrine:

00:10:12.290 --> 00:10:16.440
Follow the natural path to victory rather
than adhering to contemporary conventional

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:18.620
wisdom
If possible, avoid war.

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:23.040
The greatest victory lies in defeating the
enemy even before a war has begun

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:28.030
But if a war begins, the best way to achieve
peace is through a swift victory.

00:10:28.030 --> 00:10:32.420
These precepts of Sun Tzu’s were soon put
the test in one of the many conflicts opposing

00:10:32.420 --> 00:10:34.750
the kingdoms of Wu and Chu.

00:10:34.750 --> 00:10:38.660
At the decisive Battle of Boju, Sun-Tzu is
said to have led the Wu forces along with

00:10:38.660 --> 00:10:44.070
King He-Lu’s brother Fugai, and defeated
the numerically superior Chu army through

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:45.110
use of his tactics.

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:47.940
In The Art of War, Sun-Tzu writes:
“Though according to my estimate, the soldiers

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:53.230
of Chu exceed our own in number, that shall
advantage them nothing in the matter of victory.

00:10:53.230 --> 00:10:55.840
I say then that victory can be achieved.

00:10:55.840 --> 00:10:59.830
Though the enemy be stronger in numbers, we
may prevent him from fighting.”

00:10:59.830 --> 00:11:04.300
In other words: take the initiative; attack
first; attack fast; attack where the enemy

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:07.660
is weak and prevent him from concentrating
his forces.

00:11:07.660 --> 00:11:09.870
This is exactly what happened at Boju.

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:14.760
Despite He-Lu’s protestations, Fugai listened
to Sun Tzu’s advice and charged the Chu

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:19.300
army, defeating them in a series of five further
engagements, which culminated with the capture

00:11:19.300 --> 00:11:21.300
of the enemy capital, Ying.

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:26.940
Fugai’s success in the Wu-Chu wars was due
to his own courage and his belief in the precepts

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:27.940
of Sun Tzu.

00:11:27.940 --> 00:11:33.000
First, he collected intelligence brought by
spies, through which he learned that the opposing

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:37.630
general, Nang Wa, was despised by his troops
and that they had no will to fight.

00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:41.610
Second, he refused to adhere to the standard
rules of war as understood at that time.

00:11:41.610 --> 00:11:45.020
He did not let the enemy retreat to safety,
he attacked them while they were crossing

00:11:45.020 --> 00:11:50.140
the Qing-fa river, prevented their formation
of lines, and even later attacked them at

00:11:50.140 --> 00:11:51.140
their dinner!

00:11:51.140 --> 00:11:54.870
You may have noticed something in this account
of the Battle of Boju: that the main action

00:11:54.870 --> 00:11:59.570
was led by Fugai, with Sun Tzu being almost
a shadow, in the background.

00:11:59.570 --> 00:12:04.190
You see, it interesting to note that we do
have precise historical records of other Generals

00:12:04.190 --> 00:12:08.410
at King He-Lu’s court, but nothing so precise
about Master Sun Wu himself.

00:12:08.410 --> 00:12:14.160
It may have been, in fact that Master Sun
was exactly that, a mere phantom, an embodiment

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:16.720
of a set of values rather than a real person.

00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:21.870
Or, if we want to stay more grounded, Master
Sun may be a composite character, the result

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:28.180
of different historical figures merged into
one by inaccurate historical records or oral

00:12:28.180 --> 00:12:29.180
tradition.

00:12:29.180 --> 00:12:33.700
Two characters could plausibly be at the origin
of the legend of Sun Tzu: Wu Zixu and Sun

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:40.450
Bing.

00:12:40.450 --> 00:12:43.410
Wu Zixu
Wu Zixu was born some time at the end of the

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:47.920
VIth Century and was a son of a nobleman called
Wu She, from the state of Chu.

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:53.450
When Wu She was executed for an alleged crime,
Wu Zixu fled Chu and after wandering through

00:12:53.450 --> 00:12:55.680
the Warring States he finally he came to Wu.

00:12:55.680 --> 00:13:00.560
Here, he became acquainted with one Prince
Guang, to whom he suggested usurping the throne.

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:05.950
Guang killed the rightful King Liao and became
our now good old friend, King He-lu.

00:13:05.950 --> 00:13:10.140
Wu Zixu became He-lu's chief advisor.

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:11.779
Advisor to the King, like Sun Tzu?

00:13:11.779 --> 00:13:12.779
Check!

00:13:12.779 --> 00:13:16.250
Wu Zixu then took advantage of his position
of power to take revenge for the unjust death

00:13:16.250 --> 00:13:20.920
of his father, led a campaign against the
state of Chu and conquered the rival state

00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:25.830
in 506.

00:13:25.830 --> 00:13:27.080
Conquering the capital city of Chu, like Sun
Tzu with Fugai?

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:28.080
Second Check!

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:30.980
For his military success he was rewarded with
the territory of Shen.

00:13:30.980 --> 00:13:34.770
But with He-Lu’s death, his fortunes started
to decline.

00:13:34.770 --> 00:13:39.431
Wu Zixu did not see eye-to-eye with the new
King Fucha, who after defeating the state

00:13:39.431 --> 00:13:44.440
of Yue and Qi, wanted to go for the full hegemony
of the whole of China.

00:13:44.440 --> 00:13:49.590
Wu Zixu advised against that, as he was aware
of the limited power of the kingdom of Wu.

00:13:49.590 --> 00:13:53.149
Eventually, King Fucha favoured another general,
Bo-Pi, over him.

00:13:53.149 --> 00:13:57.050
Slandered by the new favourite, Wu Zixu accepted
a sword presented by the king, with which

00:13:57.050 --> 00:14:06.500
he killed himself in the year 484BC
Sun Bin

00:14:06.500 --> 00:14:11.020
Sun Bin, also known as Sun Ping, was rumoured
in life to be a descendant to the legendary

00:14:11.020 --> 00:14:12.040
Sun Tzu.

00:14:12.040 --> 00:14:16.200
He lived between the years 380 and 316BC in
the state of Wei.

00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:21.310
He studied military leadership under two great
generals of the period, Pang Juan and Master

00:14:21.310 --> 00:14:22.310
Guiguzi.

00:14:22.310 --> 00:14:27.390
But the old teacher, Pang Juan, became envious
of his pupils and he slandered his reputation.

00:14:27.390 --> 00:14:31.450
This was quite common at the time, it seems,
and always with dire consequences: the King

00:14:31.450 --> 00:14:34.240
of Wei punished Sun Bin by cutting off his
kneecaps.

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:38.850
The word ‘Bin’ in fact was not part of
his name at birth, it rather indicates this

00:14:38.850 --> 00:14:42.970
type of punishment and was a nickname given
to him later in his life.

00:14:42.970 --> 00:14:46.600
Somehow, Sun Bin managed to escape to the
northern state of Qi, where he was hired as

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:49.810
a general and led successful campaigns against
Wei.

00:14:49.810 --> 00:14:54.100
His preferred tactics are a close match with
some of those found in the Art of War, such

00:14:54.100 --> 00:14:58.770
as encircling the enemy forces and attacking
the weaker enemy formations, which require

00:14:58.770 --> 00:15:00.480
more support from other units.

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:05.779
After his successes with Qi, he retired to
the state of Chu, where he probably died.

00:15:05.779 --> 00:15:09.470
What makes him a candidate to be the “real
life” Sun Tzu, or at least one of the characters

00:15:09.470 --> 00:15:13.860
that inspired his legend, is that Sun Bin
authored his own Art of War!

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:18.240
This book, titled “The Art of Warfare by
Sun Bin” was discovered by archaeologists

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:24.120
in 1972 and it reads like an expansion to
the original book attributed to Sun Tzu.

00:15:24.120 --> 00:15:28.100
But whatever the truth about the identity
or existence of Master Sun Wu, the man’s

00:15:28.100 --> 00:15:33.200
life will always be secondary to his greatest
achievement, a short book in thirteen brief

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:37.490
chapters, which can be read in little more
than an hour, and yet charged with a relevance

00:15:37.490 --> 00:15:39.720
that has spanned centuries.

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:45.760
The Art of War

00:15:45.760 --> 00:15:49.910
What we know for certain about this book is
that it was first written in the Vth Century

00:15:49.910 --> 00:15:53.450
BC, later amended in the IInd Century BC.

00:15:53.450 --> 00:15:57.580
It later spread to most of East Asia, gaining
notoriety especially in Japan, around the

00:15:57.580 --> 00:15:58.760
VIIIth Century AD.

00:15:58.760 --> 00:16:03.339
We know of at least one Japanese warlord who
used as an emblem the four Chinese characters

00:16:03.339 --> 00:16:08.459
of Wind, Forest, Fire and Mountain, which
represent a famous maxim from Sun Tzu’s

00:16:08.459 --> 00:16:14.040
work: Strike like the wind, be as tranquil
as a forest, be as devastating as a fire and

00:16:14.040 --> 00:16:15.660
as firm as a mountain.

00:16:15.660 --> 00:16:20.019
The book was finally formalised in its current
version in the XIth century AD, but we had

00:16:20.019 --> 00:16:25.519
to wait until 1772 for it to be introduced
to the west, thanks to the translation of

00:16:25.519 --> 00:16:27.220
a French Jesuit Priest.

00:16:27.220 --> 00:16:31.579
The Art of War rests on a few key principles,
some of which we have already hinted at.

00:16:31.579 --> 00:16:35.460
The main point, which is a stern piece of
advice to all rulers, is that war should not

00:16:35.460 --> 00:16:39.649
be taken lightly, it is a matter of life and
death for the State and the people living

00:16:39.649 --> 00:16:40.649
within it.

00:16:40.649 --> 00:16:43.290
At least, it should be avoided at all costs.

00:16:43.290 --> 00:16:48.510
If this is not possible, it should be extensively
planned and prepared, and concluded as swiftly

00:16:48.510 --> 00:16:49.510
as possible.

00:16:49.510 --> 00:16:52.730
No state ever benefited from a prolonged state
of war.

00:16:52.730 --> 00:16:56.200
If you want to know more about Sun Tzu’s
philosophy of warfare, I strongly encourage

00:16:56.200 --> 00:17:00.050
you to go and read it, you can read it or
listen to it for free basically everywhere

00:17:00.050 --> 00:17:01.050
online.

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:06.189
But if you can’t spare one hour of your
precious time, I am going to summarise it

00:17:06.189 --> 00:17:11.589
for you in five minutes.

00:17:11.589 --> 00:17:14.439
Countdown please.

00:17:14.439 --> 00:17:15.779
Chapter 1 – Planning.

00:17:15.779 --> 00:17:20.579
The five key factors of war are: strategy,
weather, terrain, leadership, management.

00:17:20.579 --> 00:17:24.619
By considering these factors and comparing
them to the enemy a commander can calculate

00:17:24.619 --> 00:17:26.549
chances for victory.

00:17:26.549 --> 00:17:29.809
Chapter 2 - Waging War, or the economy of
warfare.

00:17:29.809 --> 00:17:34.429
Success lies in the ability to win quickly
and effectively, thus limiting the cost of

00:17:34.429 --> 00:17:35.720
military campaigns

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:40.679
Chapter 3 – Strategic Attack, in which the
Master tells us that strength comes from unity,

00:17:40.679 --> 00:17:42.840
not size.

00:17:42.840 --> 00:17:44.639
Four – Disposition of the Army.

00:17:44.639 --> 00:17:49.049
Obvious but true: about the importance of
being able to recognize strategic opportunities

00:17:49.049 --> 00:17:52.480
and to not create opportunities for the enemy.

00:17:52.480 --> 00:17:54.010
Five – Forces!

00:17:54.010 --> 00:17:58.230
About using creativity and timing to build
your army’s momentum.

00:17:58.230 --> 00:18:00.549
Six – Weaknesses and Strengths.

00:18:00.549 --> 00:18:06.570
This is Taoism at its best: always adapt your
tactics to changes in the environment.

00:18:06.570 --> 00:18:08.110
Seven – Military Manoeuvres.

00:18:08.110 --> 00:18:14.289
About the dangers of direct conflict and how
to win if you cannot avoid it: use initiative

00:18:14.289 --> 00:18:18.960
to impose your will on the enemy and fight
at your own conditions.

00:18:18.960 --> 00:18:21.539
Eight - Variations and Adaptability.

00:18:21.539 --> 00:18:24.039
Taoism, 2nd round.

00:18:24.039 --> 00:18:26.669
Adapt your tactics to the enemy army’s responses.

00:18:26.669 --> 00:18:28.889
Nine – Movement and development of troops.

00:18:28.889 --> 00:18:33.549
Or: how to evaluate the intentions of the
enemy while moving across their territory.

00:18:33.549 --> 00:18:36.450
Ten – Terrain and Eleven – The Nine Battlegrounds.

00:18:36.450 --> 00:18:40.700
Both chapters discuss how to interact with
different types of ground to your advantage,

00:18:40.700 --> 00:18:42.879
at strategic and tactical level.

00:18:42.879 --> 00:18:47.409
For example, at strategic level, if positioned
at an intersection between two or more factions,

00:18:47.409 --> 00:18:49.929
or states, always seek an alliance with at
least one of them.

00:18:49.929 --> 00:18:54.750
A tactical example: when encountering high
ground, either occupy first and hold it.

00:18:54.750 --> 00:18:57.490
If you can’t, avoid it at any cost.

00:18:57.490 --> 00:18:59.370
Twelve – Attack by fire.

00:18:59.370 --> 00:19:04.299
There are five ways to do so: burn the soldiers,
burn their stores, burn their baggage trains,

00:19:04.299 --> 00:19:08.700
burn their arsenals, shoot fire missiles from
the distance.

00:19:08.700 --> 00:19:10.090
Thirteen – Espionage.

00:19:10.090 --> 00:19:15.259
There are five types of spies: locals spying
on the enemy; infiltrated spies; double agents;

00:19:15.259 --> 00:19:20.090
spies sowing false intelligence on purpose;
and surviving spies – basically those who

00:19:20.090 --> 00:19:22.070
make it back alive to your camp.

00:19:22.070 --> 00:19:25.429
Foreknowledge and deception are key to the
conduct of war.

00:19:25.429 --> 00:19:30.039
In fact, one of the most quoted maxims of
Master Sun’s is exactly that,

00:19:30.039 --> 00:19:50.090
‘All war is based

00:19:50.090 --> 00:20:10.010
on deception’.

